#
# D-142 — FIVE PACKAGE-WIDE RULES PASSED WHEN THEIR SCAN SAW NOTHING.
#
# Every one of them was `assert not offenders` over a helper that returned
# offenders and nothing else. That assertion is green in TWO different worlds:
# the one where the corpus is clean, and the one where the derivation has
# quietly stopped recognising the corpus's spelling. The probe that filed the
# defect replaced each per-module scan with `lambda p: []` and watched all four
# of this file's rules pass. They could not even report what they had seen.
#
# The planted-copy tests do not close it. They prove the detector recognises a
# PLANTED spelling, not that it still recognises the REAL package's — and the
# failure mode is precisely a refactor that hoists "state.json" or the rename
# idiom into a constant, which empties every scan while every plant still
# passes. The suite's own comment records that this fired for real during
# GRIND-20 when the manifest reads moved behind a primitive.
#
# THE FIX: every scan returns `(members_seen, offenders)`, and every rule
# asserts `seen` includes the sites the defect was filed on, BY NAME.
#
#   state.json's cycle   the guarded readers are now RECOGNISED and merely
#                        skipped when deciding who offends, instead of being
#                        `continue`d past before looking. `_current_cycle` and
#                        `_server_cycle` are where every read of the counter
#                        now lives, so they are the anchor.
#   document loads       the members are the READS -- "the read and the decode
#                        are one operation" is the sentence the rule is built
#                        on -- and all fourteen D-137 sites converged on
#                        `foundry_state.read_text_file`.
#   artifact renames     the rename sites are collected whether or not the
#                        module locks; the lock only decides who offends. The
#                        old shape returned [] on sight of a `flock`, so the
#                        two modules that OWN the write primitive contributed
#                        nothing to anchor on.
#   ledger iterations    `foundry_sync_defects` and `foundry_mark_defect_fixed`
#                        still open transactions, so the scan must still see
#                        them.
#
# `test_spawn_progress` applies the identical change to the manifest-scan
# family; the plain `(seen, offenders)` tuple is the agreed contract between
# the two files.
#
# WHAT THE TABLE SHOWS: each rule reporting "none" WHILE naming the member it
# can still see -- "clean" and "blind" print differently now, which is the
# whole of this defect -- then a fresh copy of every closed shape planted where
# a directory literal could not reach it, each one named. The pytest line below
# includes the blind-taint probes: each rule driven with its own recogniser
# tainted to see nothing, asserted to FAIL on its anchor.
